# Copy to .github/CODEOWNERS.
#
# This file is the other half of "owned by name" in AGENTS.md. A context file without a
# named owner rots inside a quarter, and a rotted context file is worse than none, because
# it teaches the agent things about your codebase that are no longer true.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CODEOWNERS BLOCKS NOTHING UNTIL THE REPOSITORY IS CONFIGURED TO LET IT.
#
# Three settings, under Settings -> Branches -> branch protection rule for your default
# branch:
#
#   1. Require a pull request before merging
#   2. Require review from Code Owners
#   3. Require status checks to pass -> add `tier1` and ONLY `tier1`
#
# On (3): never add the tier-2 job. Agents are non-deterministic — the same pull request
# re-run can produce a different verdict — and a gate that flakes destroys trust in about a
# week. `continue-on-error: true` in the workflow is the belt; keeping tier2 out of this
# list is the brace. Neither alone is sufficient: if someone adds tier2 here, it blocks
# regardless of what the workflow says.
#
# All three need repository admin, and agreeing to them needs the team. That is the honest
# boundary of what a lecture can hand you — everything above this line is one pull request,
# and this part is a conversation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Default owner for anything not matched below.
*                           @your-org/your-team

# The context file has a named owner, and changes go through review. This line is the
# whole mechanism.
/AGENTS.md                  @your-org/tech-leads

# The gates. Changing what blocks a merge should not be a solo decision.
/.pre-commit-config.yaml    @your-org/tech-leads
/.importlinter              @your-org/tech-leads
/.semgrep/                  @your-org/tech-leads
/.github/workflows/         @your-org/tech-leads

# Team review standards live in a file, so changing them is a pull request and the
# disagreement happens once, in writing, instead of every sprint in review comments.
/.github/skills/            @your-org/tech-leads

# Per-service context files in a monorepo. The agent reads the nearest one, so the team
# that owns the service owns its context.
/services/billing/AGENTS.md @your-org/billing
/services/search/AGENTS.md  @your-org/search
